# Data Retention and Deletion Standard — R-4 Legal Hold

> Module LegalHold

This module answers two questions: does a Legal Hold suspend deletion of a
record, and when does deletion fall due once the hold is released?

R-4.1 is expressed as "notwithstanding R-3", so the hold override cannot live
inside `Retention`: it must defeat whatever R-3 limb produced the retention
end date. `retention_end_date` therefore arrives here as an input — it is
`Retention.RetentionEnd.retention_end_date` — and the override sits at the
bottom of the exception chain on the deletion decision, below every other
limb.

`deletion_due_date` is an `optional of date`. `Absent` means **no deletion
date falls due**, which is the only correct answer while a hold is in force:
R-4.1 forbids deletion irrespective of expiry of the retention period, so
reporting the expired retention date as a due date would invite exactly the
deletion the clause prohibits.

## Prologue — declarations

`retention_under_hold_permitted_by_data_protection_law` is the **judgement
input** of R-4.2. Whether applicable data protection law permits continued
retention against a particular erasure request is not computable from
anything in this Standard; encoded as a plain rule the module would assert
that a hold always defeats an erasure request.

A hold is described by two recorded dates: `hold_issued_date` (`Absent` where
no hold was ever issued) and `hold_released_date` (`Absent` where the hold
has not been released). `assessment_date` is the date as at which the
questions are asked, since a hold released last month was in force the month
before.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope HoldEffect:
  input hold_issued_date content optional of date
  input hold_released_date content optional of date
  input retention_end_date content date
  input assessment_date content date
  input retention_under_hold_permitted_by_data_protection_law content boolean
  output hold_in_force content boolean
  output deletion_due_date content optional of date
  output erasure_blocked_by_hold content boolean
```

| NO-CLAUSE: the rounding mode is a compiler directive, not a rule. Only day
| durations are added in this scope (R-4.3's 30 days), which are never
| ambiguous, but the mode is declared so that the scope's arithmetic is
| total by construction rather than by inspection. `date round down` matches
| `Retention`, whose R-3 result this scope consumes, so a single record
| cannot be rounded one way here and the other way there.

```catala
scope HoldEffect:
  date round down
```

## R-2 Definitions

| DATA-RET R-2.3 (006-data-retention-standard.md:34)
|
| "Legal Hold" means a documented instruction issued by the General Counsel
| or their delegate to preserve specified records in connection with actual
| or reasonably anticipated litigation, investigation or regulatory
| proceedings.

The clause's evaluative element — whether litigation is "reasonably
anticipated" — is exercised by the General Counsel *before* issuing the
instruction, so what this module consumes is a documented instruction with a
date, not a judgement it has to make. That is why R-4.1 below is RULE and not
HYBRID.

A hold is in force as at the assessment date if it has been issued on or
before that date and not yet released by it. A hold is spent on the day it is
released, which is what makes R-4.3's 30 days start running.

```catala
scope HoldEffect:
  label r2_3 definition hold_in_force equals
    match hold_issued_date with pattern
    -- Absent: false
    -- Present content issued:
         assessment_date >= issued
         and (match hold_released_date with pattern
              -- Absent: true
              -- Present content released: assessment_date < released)
```

## R-4 Legal Hold

| NO-CLAUSE: the base case of the deletion decision is the ordinary retention
| expiry that R-3 fixes, and R-3 is encoded in `Retention`, not here — this
| block adds no law of its own, it only names that module's result as the
| base that R-4.3 and R-4.1 are exceptions to. Encoding R-3 again here would
| put one clause in two modules.

```catala
scope HoldEffect:
  label r3_ordinary definition deletion_due_date
    equals Present content retention_end_date
```

| DATA-RET R-4.3 (006-data-retention-standard.md:75)
|
| On release of a Legal Hold, the record is deleted within 30 days if its
| retention period has already expired, and otherwise on the ordinary expiry
| of its retention period.

Two branches on one condition — that a hold was issued and has since been
released. Which branch applies turns on whether the retention period had
*already* expired when the hold was released, so the comparison is against
the release date and not against today: a record whose period expires next
year is deleted next year, however long the hold ran.

The boundary is exclusive. A period that expires on the very day the hold is
released has not "already" expired, so that record falls in the second branch
and is deleted on its ordinary expiry, which is that same day.

```catala
scope HoldEffect:
  label r4_3 exception r3_ordinary definition deletion_due_date
    under condition
      (hold_issued_date with pattern Present)
      and (hold_released_date with pattern Present)
    consequence equals
      match hold_released_date with pattern
      -- Absent: Present content retention_end_date
      -- Present content released:
           if retention_end_date < released
           then Present content (released + 30 day)
           else Present content retention_end_date
```

| DATA-RET R-4.1 (006-data-retention-standard.md:68)
|
| Notwithstanding R-3, a record subject to a Legal Hold must not be deleted
| while the Legal Hold remains in force, irrespective of the expiry of the
| applicable retention period.

"Notwithstanding R-3" puts this clause below everything else, so it is an
exception to `r4_3`, which is itself an exception to the R-3 base. It
therefore defeats every R-3 limb and R-4.3 alike, which is what the words
require: no deletion date falls due while the hold is in force, *irrespective
of the expiry* of the retention period.

Nothing is repeated from the parent's condition here, and deliberately so.
R-4.1 is not a qualification of R-4.3; it applies on its own terms whenever a
hold is in force. It overlaps `r4_3` only as at an assessment date falling
between issue and release of a hold that has since been released, and in that
overlap R-4.1 must win — the hold was in force then. Sitting below `r4_3` in
the chain is what secures that.

```catala
scope HoldEffect:
  label r4_1 exception r4_3 definition deletion_due_date
    under condition hold_in_force
    consequence equals Absent
```

| DATA-RET R-4.2 (006-data-retention-standard.md:72)
|
| A Legal Hold takes precedence over any request for erasure made by a data
| subject, to the extent permitted by applicable data protection law.

**HYBRID.** The precedence is determinate — a hold in force blocks an erasure
request — but it holds only "to the extent permitted by applicable data
protection law", which is the judgement input. The limitation is encoded as
an exception to the precedence rather than folded into a conjunction, so that
the clause's two parts stay separately visible: the rule, and the legal limit
on it.

The exception repeats the parent's operative term `hold_in_force`. The parent
is unconditional, so this is not strictly required to keep the exception in
its parent's domain, but it keeps the limitation from reading as a statement
about records under no hold at all.

`erasure_blocked_by_hold` is the value `Erasure_request` consumes for the
third limb of R-5.2, so the R-4.2 judgement cannot be bypassed by reading the
hold's existence directly.

```catala
scope HoldEffect:
  label r4_2 definition erasure_blocked_by_hold equals hold_in_force

  label r4_2_permitted exception r4_2 definition erasure_blocked_by_hold
    under condition
      hold_in_force
      and not retention_under_hold_permitted_by_data_protection_law
    consequence equals false
```
